WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.520
- American electric vehicle
companies were the biggest

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.740
things since sliced bread
just a couple years ago.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.990
Names like Lordstown Motors, Fisker,

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.720
and Nikola were once hailed
as the next big thing.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.310
The heir apparent to Tesla

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.950
and the companies that were
gonna usher in the next wave

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.270
of the American car.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.550
Well, that hasn't happened at all.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.650
EV companies that had valuations

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.630
that rivaled American auto giants Ford

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:22.980
and GM are now on the verge

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.080
of seeing their stock prices go to zero.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.730
I'm going to explain what's happening

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.760
to these high flying electric
vehicle companies, why many

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.590
of them are on the verge of insolvency

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.800
and what this means for the
future of the American car.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.330
Let's get into it.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.670
What's happening to American EV companies?

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.740
Tesla has done extraordinarily well.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.110
It's the most valuable
car company in the world,

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.020
and its stock is up more
than 100% year to date.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.260
But the other EV companies
have fallen apart

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.550
over the past year or so.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.730
One extreme example, hydrogen
electric vehicle startup

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.140
Nikola had a valuation of
nearly $29 billion in 2020

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.700
that made it one of the most valuable

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.230
car companies in the US.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.340
Not anymore, Nikola stock
has fallen by around 95%

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.920
and the company is worth
around a billion dollars today.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.500
Its stock has never fully
recovered from a short sellers

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.330
report filled with accusations,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.790
including against founder Trevor Milton.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.230
He was convicted of fraud

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.750
and recently sentenced
to four years in prison,

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.500
but Nikola hasn't even been
the worst performing EV stock.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.900
Companies like Lordstown, Faraday Futures

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.090
and Proterra have all
seen their stocks fall

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.880
by 99% since going public.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.530
Short sellers who make investments

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.770
that company stock prices
will fall, have piled in

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.180
and gotten paid.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.320
Faraday, Nikola

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.290
and Proterra have been the
best performing short bets in

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.120
the automotive sector all year.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.480
My colleague George Downs covers
transportation and he says,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.670
- Making cars is hard.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.160
There is no simpler way I can say that.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.770
- George, what's going on in the EV space?

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.940
- So when a lot of these
companies went public in

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.800
2020, 2021, many legacy automakers had yet

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.410
to release their kind
of flagship EV models.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.830
So production of Ford's F-150
Lightning hadn't started yet.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.400
These startups didn't have any
existing internal combustion

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.290
engine production lines that they needed

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.030
to maintain or pivot from.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.490
So it seemed that they
could possibly bring an EV

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.710
to market quicker and easier than some

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.090
of the legacy car companies.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.010
The problem is whether
you're a young startup

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.920
or an old automaker,
engineering is engineering,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.450
manufacturing is manufacturing,

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.840
and supply chains are supply chains,

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.840
there are no real shortcuts.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.960
And even Tesla's CEO Elon Musk
said that the company went

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.720
through manufacturing hell in 2017,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.900
supply chains got snarled for
pretty much every automaker

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.480
and startups struggled to get
the parts that they needed.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.040
And every day that you're
not getting a vehicle off the

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.350
production line and selling
it to customers is a day

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.910
that you're potentially losing money.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.840
- But data show Americans
do want electric cars.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.150
EV sales continue to rise in the US

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.250
and are seeing exponential growth,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.500
Analytics firm, Cox
Automotive estimates that

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.530
around 300,000 electric
vehicles were sold in the third

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.560
quarter, making up around
8% of all auto sales.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.510
That's more than double
the number of sales

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.360
and shares seen in the
third quarter of 2021

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.830
and four times the sales

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.020
and shares seen in the
third quarter of 2020.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.110
So what does the future look
like for electric vehicles?

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.060
A report from our
colleague, Amrith Ramkumar

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.830
and Shane Shifflett recently found that

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.130
of 43 public electric vehicle
companies they studied,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.470
18 were on track to run
out of cash by the end

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.680
of next year if they didn't
cut costs or raise new capital

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.590
and seven of those had
just weeks of cash on hand.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.060
When contacted by the journal,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.740
several companies said they raised

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.010
or are planning new financing
and or cutting costs.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.370
So is it game over for EV
companies here in the US?

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.650
- I think what you're seeing
now is the hype starting

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.870
to fade away, but reality setting in.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:45.120
So the companies that are in
a stronger position are the

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.370
ones that have actually
managed to get their EVs

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.110
into the hands of paying customers.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.150
But even the big players are reassessing.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.830
GM abandoned its target to
build 400,000 EVs by 2024,

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.170
and Ford has pushed back its targets

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.690
as our latest WSJ reporting points out.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:04.140
There's still a bunch of
companies losing a lot of money,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.490
so it's likely there's
gonna be more bloodshed.

